<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Merneptah Stele</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1208 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a> · Also known as Israel Stele</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merneptah">Merneptah</a> Stele is an inscribed granite slab of the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> Merneptah, set up around 1208 BCE and found at Thebes in 1896. It records his victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libyans">Libyans</a> and their allies and closes with a hymn listing defeated places in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ashkelon</a>, Gezer and Yenoam. It contains the earliest known mention of Israel and is now in the Egyptian Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/merneptah-stele.jpg" alt="Merneptah Stele" width="320" height="628" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 𐰇𐱅𐰚𐰤 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Merneptah_Steli_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The stele is a black granite slab, over 3 metres high, carved in the fifth year of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merneptah">Merneptah</a> of the 19th Dynasty. Its text is engraved on the rough back of a stele of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii">Amenhotep III</a>, which had been set against the wall in the forecourt of Merneptah's own temple. Flinders Petrie found it at Thebes in 1896, and Wilhelm Spiegelberg of his team made the first translation. At the top, the scene keeps its original colouring and shows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> giving a sword to the king, with Mut and Khonsu on either side. A fragmentary copy was found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> had been the dominant power in the region under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merneptah">Merneptah</a>'s fifth year a Libu king invaded from the west in alliance with northern peoples, and Merneptah won a great victory that summer. Most of the 28 lines glorify this victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libyans">Libyans</a> and their Sea People allies.</p><p>The last three lines turn to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>, then part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s imperial possessions, where some cities had revolted. They say that Asqaluni was carried off, Gezer seized, Yanoam made non-existent, and Israel laid waste, its seed no more. The phrase about the seed is formulaic and was often used of defeated nations. The three cities are written with the determinative for a city, while Israel carries the signs for a foreign people, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> typically used for groups without a fixed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> home.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>When Spiegelberg was puzzled by one group of signs, Petrie quickly proposed that it read Israel, and Spiegelberg agreed. A majority of scholars accept the reading, and mainstream scholarship sees a connection with biblical Israel, although minority readings such as Jezreel have been proposed. It is the earliest textual reference to Israel and the only one from ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Most scholars place <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merneptah">Merneptah</a>'s Israel in the hill country of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>.</p><p>In the 1970s Frank J. Yurco argued that reliefs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> long assigned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> belong to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merneptah">Merneptah</a> and show the campaign; not all Egyptologists accept this, and Dan'el Kahn has argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> appear as Shasu nomads in other scenes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Merneptah Stele” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Thutmose III lists Gezer among his conquests, its kings Milkilu and Yapahu wrote Amarna letters, and the <b>Merneptah Stele</b> boasts that Gezer was seized.</blockquote><a href="/state/gezer">Gezer</a></li></ul></section>
